// Azure DevOps audit events that represent a GOVERNANCE CHANGE - permissions, policies,
// group membership, service connections, agents - with the execution noise removed.
//
// Requires the organization's audit log to be STREAMED to this workspace (Organization
// Settings > Auditing > Streams). Without the stream the table does not exist, and the ADO
// audit API itself keeps only 90 days - the stream is what makes this durable, because the
// retention then becomes the workspace's rather than ADO's.
//
// READING AzureDevOpsAuditing UNFILTERED TELLS YOU ALMOST NOTHING. Measured on one
// organization over 30 days: 68,953 events, of which 30,096 were Library/Execute (a pipeline
// reading a variable group) and 16,715 Pipelines/Execute. The security-relevant remainder is
// roughly 2,000 - under 3%.
//
// CATEGORY ALONE DOES NOT SEPARATE NOISE FROM SIGNAL, which is the trap that makes a naive
// "where Category != 'Execute'" look like it worked. Pipelines.RunRetained and
// Pipelines.RunUnretained are Category == "Modify" and are pure run-retention bookkeeping -
// 9,384 of the 11,000 non-Execute events on the same organization. They are excluded by
// OPERATION NAME below, not by category.
//
// NO ORGANIZATION OR PROJECT FILTER IN THE QUERY. ProjectName and ScopeDisplayName are
// columns, so narrow in PowerShell - Where-Object ProjectName -eq 'Security'. One workspace
// can receive streams from several organizations; a filter baked in here means a file per
// organization and a stale one the day a project is renamed.
//
// Git.RefUpdatePoliciesBypassed IS THE ONE TO READ FIRST. It records a push that bypassed
// branch policy - 168 events by 7 actors in 30 days on the measured organization - and is the
// only row here that describes a control being deliberately stepped around rather than
// reconfigured.
//
// ActorUPN is empty for service principals and build identities, and ActorDisplayName is a
// GUID for them rather than a name - measured, the single busiest actor in 30 days resolved to
// neither until it was looked up with Get-MsecAzureDevOpsUser. Actor is best-effort; treat a
// GUID as "a build identity, not yet resolved" rather than as an account.
//
// THERE IS NO BYPASS REASON IN THIS DATA, and looking for one is how an afternoon disappears.
// Git.RefUpdatePoliciesBypassed records a PUSH that bypassed policy; Azure DevOps prompts for
// a justification on PULL REQUEST completion, which is a different event that this table did
// not contain at all over the measured 30 days. Data carries repo, branch and the before/after
// commit ids and nothing else - identical for human and service identities. The nearest thing
// to "why" is the commit message behind NewObjectId, which has to be fetched from the Git API.
//
// Repo, Branch and NewCommit are lifted out of Data because the bare Details sentence cannot
// be filtered or joined on.
//
// AN ALL-ZERO COMMIT ID IS A DELETION, NOT A PUSH, and reading one as the other overstates
// what happened. Git uses 0000...0 as the null object id: all-zero NewObjectId means the ref
// was DELETED, all-zero OldObjectId means it was CREATED. Measured over 30 days, 6 of the 14
// human policy bypasses were branch deletions rather than pushes - a merged feature branch
// being tidied away, not somebody forcing code past review. RefChange says which, and is
// empty for operations that do not touch a ref at all rather than claiming "pushed".
AzureDevOpsAuditing
| where Category !in ("Execute", "Access")
// Run bookkeeping, not governance - see the header.
| where OperationName !startswith "Pipelines.Run"
| where OperationName !in ("Pipelines.ResourceAuthorizedForPipeline", "Release.ReleaseCreated")
| extend Actor = iff(isempty(ActorUPN), ActorDisplayName, ActorUPN)
| extend Repo      = tostring(Data.RepoName),
         Branch    = tostring(Data.FriendlyName),
         OldCommit = tostring(Data.OldObjectId),
         NewCommit = tostring(Data.NewObjectId)
// Empty, not "pushed", for anything that does not touch a ref - see the header.
| extend RefChange = case(isempty(NewCommit) and isempty(OldCommit), "",
                          NewCommit matches regex "^0+$", "deleted",
                          OldCommit matches regex "^0+$", "created",
                          "pushed")
| project TimeGenerated, Actor, OperationName, Area, Category,
          ProjectName, Repo, Branch, RefChange, OldCommit, NewCommit,
          ScopeType, ScopeDisplayName, IpAddress, Details, CorrelationId
| order by TimeGenerated desc
